  "grammar": {
    "title": "Adverbs of Frequency (always, usually, never)",
    "explanation": "**Adverbs of frequency** say **how often** something happens. **Always** means 100% of the time, and **never** means 0%.\n\nThe adverb goes **before** the main verb. You can talk **about** your week: **usually** fly on Monday, **always** watch an action film on Friday.\n\nAfter the verb **be** (am, is, are), the adverb goes **after** it: **is always**, **are usually**.\n\nAsk with **How often...?** Answer with an adverb, or put a time expression like **twice a week** at the **end**.",
    "examples": [
      "*I **always** have coffee in the morning.* (before the main verb)",
      "*We **usually** go to the beach in summer.* (before the main verb)",
      "*She **often** reads before bed.* (before the main verb)",
      "*My brother is **never** late.* (after be)",
      "*He **doesn't usually** work on Saturdays.* (after doesn't)",
      "*How often do you see your grandparents? – **Once a month**.* (frequency expression)"
    ],
